  "body": "The conjecture did not misread the rule. The rule says \"The first refusal phrase the text contains\", which means earliest occurrence in the text. The code loops over REFUSAL_PHRASES in list order and returns the first list item found anywhere, so for Text A it returns \"I cannot\" even though \"I am unable\" occurs earlier in the text. Thus the rule as written and the code part company; the conjecture correctly identifies this.\n\nThe reading 26ddcc6d71fe9c2b is defective. It claims to render the conjecture's expectation as \"moves\" and supplies a rewrite \"I cannot do that.\", but the conjecture never says the kernel moves or rewrites anything. The conjecture says the check \"gives the same answer for two texts\" and states what would make it wrong: \"if running the check on Text A returns 'I am unable' rather than 'I cannot', or if the two texts produce different answers from the check.\" That is an expectation of unchanged output, not a move. The reading is wrong, not the conjecture; the invented \"moves\" expectation is not in the prose.\n\nA next conjecture should try a single input where the earliest refusal phrase is not first in REFUSAL_PHRASES, e.g. \"I am unable to help. I cannot do that.\", and assert the kernel returns \"I am unable\". It should avoid repeating the two-text comparison and the \"moves\" rewrite expectation, because the kernel returns a phrase or None, not a rewritten text.",
